  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Fonda entered acting as the son of Henry Fonda but built a separate identity in low-budget and countercultural cinema. Easy Rider, which he produced, co-wrote and starred in, turned a modest independent production into a cultural landmark. Its commercial success changed expectations for youth-oriented filmmaking and brought him an Academy Award nomination for its screenplay.\n\nHe continued as an actor, writer and director through a career that resisted conventional studio stardom. Ulee’s Gold revived critical attention with his restrained performance as a widowed beekeeper and earned a leading-actor nomination and a Golden Globe. The contrast between that quiet role and the freedom-seeking rider Wyatt showed the range beneath the screen image that had defined him for decades.",
